/*
  core/interface_hardened_verify.spthy -- a first-class INTERFACE as a demand-transformer (ROADMAP #2).

  An interface is named demand profile: the set of !Demands rows it induces for the steps it presents.
  The DEFAULT/baseline interface (core/lexicon_tlx.spthy) presents a key-fingerprint COMPARE as a raw
  hex string -> high Effort -> sigma6 Abstraction fires -> Naive -> accepts a tampered key (the mistake).

  This HARDENED interface presents the same compare via a usability-improved affordance -- a side-by-side
  visual fingerprint diff / "numbers match?" prompt -- which LOWERS the Effort of the compare step to
  'lo'. sigma6 reads !Demands(_,'Effort','hi'); under this interface that row is never produced, so sigma6
  cannot fire: the user is not overwhelmed, stays Attentive, and the tampered-key mistake is UNREACHABLE.

  This is the RFC lever (ROADMAP goal): contrast a profile under this interface (mistake unreachable, see
  experiments/P7_interface) with one under the baseline (mistake reachable, e.g. P2). The proof pair yields
  the normative requirement -- "implementations MUST present the fingerprint comparison via a low-Effort
  affordance (e.g. a visual diff), not a raw hex string." Note this fixes the CAUSE (the stressor never
  fires), upstream of the Poka-Yoke mitigations (P6) which block the bad OUTCOME after the stressor fires.

  Seeded unconditionally (persistent, read-only -> closed theory, no sources loop). A profile includes
  THIS instead of (the compare rows of) lexicon_tlx to model the hardened interface.
*/

rule Seed_interface_hardened_verify:
    [ ]
  --[ SeedInterface('hardened_verify') ]->
    [ !Demands('compare', 'Effort', 'lo'),
      !Interface('hardened_verify') ]

restriction OnceHardenedVerify:
  "All #i #j. SeedInterface('hardened_verify')@i & SeedInterface('hardened_verify')@j ==> #i = #j"
